== C. does insertion order alone change which one nx.shortest_path returns? ==
   Same edge SET, two different insertion ORDERS -- nothing else differs:
     s7-uplink inserted first   -> ['10.0.0.3', 3, 7, 9, 5, 1, '10.0.0.1']   (s3 leaves via s7)
     s8-uplink inserted first   -> ['10.0.0.3', 3, 8, 9, 5, 1, '10.0.0.1']   (s3 leaves via s8)

   nx.shortest_path on an unweighted graph is BFS, and BFS breaks equal-length ties by the
   order a node's neighbours are iterated -- which for nx.DiGraph is INSERTION ORDER.
   Insertion order here is LLDP packet-in arrival order across ten concurrent gRPC receive threads.

== E. attempted live confirmation of the ordering, and why it is not here ==
   To tie a specific bring-up's chosen uplink to that bring-up's LLDP arrival order I would need the
   proxy to record add_link order or the routes it pushed. At the shipped log level it records neither:
   in .test_run/logs/p4_proxy.log (the last cycle's, 1694 lines) 'Pushing P4 rule' appears 0 times and
   the only discovery lines are three banners:
80:[Proxy Agent] Started LLDP Discovery...
84:[TopologyManager] link watchdog seeded with 32 declared links
85:[Proxy Agent] Started LLDP link watchdog...
   Getting it would mean changing proxy logging, which is out of scope for a measurement round.
   So the attribution rests on the source path plus the deterministic offline demonstration in section C,
   which reproduces exactly the two outcomes seen live. Recorded as NOT-DONE in SUMMARY.md section E.
